# ANCHORED, and the leading slash is the whole point. A bare `data/` matches a
# directory of that name at ANY depth, and it did: `web/src/data/site.ts` — the
# site's single source of truth, and the file `tests/test_governance_docs.py`
# re-derives the published counts from — was silently untracked for as long as
# the site has existed. Nothing local said so, because the maintainer's working
# copy had it; a fresh clone failed six governance tests with FileNotFoundError
# and could not build the site at all. Same lesson the `/sources/` rule below
# already learned, applied late.
#
# `evidence/` is anchored for the same reason before it costs anything:
# `project-template/evidence/` is shipped content, and a new file added there
# would have disappeared exactly as quietly.
#
# `build/` and `exports/` further down stay UNANCHORED — depth-matching IS what
# those two want, because a `build/` anywhere holds output. But the same rule
# swallowed `project-template/build/`, which is not output: it is the shipped
# skeleton `/new-project` copies into a new paper project, and without it a
# clean clone produced a project with no `build/` at all (caught by
# `tests/test_first_run.py::test_project_skeleton_directories_exist`). Its
# marker is re-included below, contents still ignored — the same
# ignore-the-contents-keep-the-directory idiom `project-template/sources/pdf/`
# already uses.
/data/
/evidence/
# Top-level sources/ is a working/test artifact in the kit repo (the shipped
# template lives under project-template/sources/, which this anchored rule
# leaves untouched). Without this, a stray sources/venues.jsonl written by a
# live venue_check.py run sits untracked and a careless `git add -A` would
# commit it — same class of leak the evidence/ rule above already closes.
/sources/
project-template/sources/pdf/*
!project-template/sources/pdf/.gitkeep
test-run/
# Real projects run WITH the kit, from inside the kit's own checkout — which is
# where they have to live while scholar-kit is not installed as a plugin, since
# /new-project only resolves from a directory that carries .claude/skills/.
# One roof rather than one line per project: these are the showcase runs across
# different fields, and a rule per run would turn this file into a listing of
# them. Each holds a full copy of the runtime (/new-project makes projects
# self-contained), so none of it belongs in this repository's history — what
# ships is the finished artifacts, hand-picked into examples/showcase/.
runs/
# A user's own screening labels, and only that file: `eval/screening/README.md`
# and `gold.example.jsonl` are shipped documentation. Anchored per the lesson
# above. Ignored rather than merely absent because a gold set committed here
# would be scored by anyone who cloned the kit, against decisions made on
# somebody else's topic — and `test_screening_score.py` asserts it never ships.
/eval/screening/gold.jsonl
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
build/
exports/
# Re-include the shipped skeleton directory only — its contents stay ignored.
# The directory itself has to be un-ignored first: git does not descend into an
# ignored directory, so a bare `!project-template/build/.gitkeep` would never be
# reached.
!project-template/build/
project-template/build/*
!project-template/build/.gitkeep
*.aux
*.log
*.log.latex
.env*
hocamdan/
# `<path>.lock` sidecars from lib_common.file_lock() — runtime artifacts of the
# cross-process write lock, never content. Scoped to the extensions the kit
# actually locks (registry/cards/venues/search-log .jsonl, references.bib,
# verify-status.json) instead of a bare `*.lock`, which also swallowed
# uv.lock/poetry.lock/Cargo.lock — dependency lockfiles that a repository is
# supposed to commit, and whose absence is a reproducibility defect rather than
# a tidy working tree.
*.jsonl.lock
*.bib.lock
*.json.lock
/pytest_run_output.txt
# Coverage data. `.coverage.*` is the parallel-mode per-process file: with
# subprocess measurement on (see .coveragerc) one lands per script invocation,
# so a run leaves hundreds of them behind until the parent combines.
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/

# Site build toolchain. `web/` is the SOURCE; the built site is committed to
# docs/ (GitHub Pages serves it from there, which is what keeps the deploy free
# of a CI workflow). node_modules and Astro's caches are never committed.
web/node_modules/
web/.astro/
web/dist/
# `astro dev` drops two of its own caches into outDir, which is docs/: the
# content-layer store and the update-check preferences. Neither is site content
# and neither is produced by `astro build`, so they only ever appear when the
# dev server has been run — a dirty tree that says nothing about the site.
docs/data-store.json
docs/settings.json
